# The memo of array's mints: one concrete word per element type, built once (DESIGN ›Representation‹:
# "a generic type is a chooser like a generic fn, each parameter yielding its own concrete identity built
# once"). It is its own identity, not a value of array, because array has no instances and no value block
# (ruled 18 September 2026): array's parse reads it as an outer name ("a top-level name read inside a body
# is the shared place itself, not a copy", DESIGN ›Memory and concurrency‹; a call is a use of every outer
# name, #125), so nothing here is per-instance data and the word `shared` stays retired.
# The list cannot be typed with array itself: `array word ()` would run array's parse while array_mints is
# still an unfilled hole (`:=` declares its name before it reads its value), so it is the tape's list type.
array_mints := word (
    mut mints := doublylinkedlist word (),   # the mints in minting order; written only from this body, where mut grants it
    find := fn (t := word ?) -> word (       # the mint for element type t, or a valueless word when none is minted yet
        for m in mints ( if m.element_type == t return m ),   # type identities are interned, so == is the exact test
        word ?
    ),
    add := fn (m := word ?) -> void ( mints.push(m) )
)
